Run a stored workflow: one launch compiles a whole pipeline of tasks, wired together with their dependencies, input bindings, and gates, and the scheduler executes it. Use this instead of filing the steps by hand whenever a procedure already exists — "the full release process", "the incident writeup", "the dependency bump" — because the stored template is the version that has been debugged and yours will not be. Name the workflow by its name (or its id) and pass `inputs` matching its declared input schema; the run's entry steps read straight from that payload. A launch is refused rather than half-done if the input or the template is wrong, and the refusal names the step and the key to fix. If you are unsure a workflow exists, launching by a wrong name comes back with the list of workflows that do.
